llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
115; needs 111): the end of a MoE layer at one token (weighted expert sum,
shared expert and residual adds, the next layer's RMS norm and the q8_1
quantize of its output) runs as one kernel.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches"). A Qwen3.6 35B-A3B decode
token with the routed experts in system RAM issues about 1,425 dispatches,
about 1,064 of them on the critical path.

At the end of every MoE layer at one token the CUDA backend runs four
kernels on single rows of 2048 floats (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B): the router-weighted
sum of the expert outputs (moe_weighted_reduction), the add of the shared
expert and the residual (one fused add), the next layer's RMS norm times
its weight (one fused kernel), and the q8_1 quantize of that result for
the matrix-vector products that read it (with 111, once per input). They
are almost all dispatch cost: 120 dispatches per token fewer on Qwen3.6
35B-A3B (40 layers).

Alone, with HIP graphs off (tg128 x5, two rounds): 26.30 -> 26.70 tok/s (+1.5%).

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in
system RAM with -ncmoe 99, tg128, 5 repetitions, page cache dropped before
each run, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1), patches 113-116
together: 25.60 -> 27.51 tok/s with HIP graphs (the default), 26.21 ->
27.93 tok/s with GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1. With this patch alone and with
all four, greedy 128-token output is byte-identical to the build without
them, and llama-perplexity with -ub 1 (every token through the decode path)
gives mean KLD 0 and the same perplexity in every digit.

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_try_fuse extends the moe_weighted_reduction match by the four
nodes after it, ADD(shared expert), ADD(residual), RMS_NORM and
MUL(weight), when all are f32, contiguous and one token of a row length
that is a multiple of 1024, and ggml_can_fuse_subgraph allows it with the
residual stream h and the normed row as outputs (main stream only).
moe_tail_rms_norm_mul_q8_1_f32 runs it as one block of 1024 threads, the
block size rms_norm_f32 uses for rows of 1024 and more: per column the
expression of moe_weighted_reduction_f32, then (moe + shared) + residual in
the fused add's order, writing h and accumulating x * x in rms_norm_f32's
thread order; the same block reduction; then scale * h * weight, written
out and quantized in the same 32-lane groups and butterfly reductions as
quantize_q8_1. h may be the shared-expert or residual tensor itself (an
in-place add); any other overlap of h with an input refuses.
graph_optimize keeps the experts, weights, shared expert and residual
allocated until the group's last node.

This depends on 111. The q8_1 copy is kept in the context as
mmvq_src1_pending and becomes 111's kept input (mmvq_src1) once the
evaluation loop has checked every node of the group for writes, which
would otherwise drop it for the group's own write of the normed row; the
next layer's first matrix-vector product then skips its quantize. 111's
kept buffer is released before the new one is allocated, so pool frees
stay in reverse allocation order as the VMM pool requires.

Numerics are unchanged: every value is bit-identical to running the four
kernels in turn (same expressions, per-thread element order, block
reduction and quantization groups). On gfx803 the reduction and x * x
compile to v_mac as in the original kernels, and v_fma appears only inside
the division expansions, as in rms_norm_f32 and quantize_q8_1.

